[Music]  hello and thank you very much indeed to  everyone who's tuned in uh to this talk  i should let you know that i am not  speaking as an mp let alone as a  government minister i'm speaking as  someone who's interested in ideas have  training in philosophy  taught the subject at one point and is  keen to think about where these  important concepts came from and what  they mean and whether we can think about  them productively for the future so uh  the talk is the virtues of commerce and  let's crack on in the first  slide and just start i think by pausing  for a moment to ask ourselves  where we are now in particular because a  global pandemic has swept uh across  uh countries and continents  uh and has brought us all into i think a  a new sense of the fragility of our own  lives perhaps or our society our nations  and perhaps our interrelationships with  each other personal and national so  kovid  forces us to think about what it is we  care about what it is we cherish and  what we wish for the future and that's  an incredibly important thing i've  called it a coveted revolution because  of course some people many people  and in particular many people from the  left of politics and of uh political  thinking and i have in mind people like  judith butler or in this country perhaps  amir sunivasan or in america katrina  forrester have thought about  this as potentially a moment where  we could take stock and  potentially  there might be in due course because for  a revolution that overturned uh the  values that they take to be  intrinsic or some of them take to be  intrinsic to our current way of life and  and of course if you think that uh  contemporary society is is um  preoccupied with and only a matter of  inequality or domination of  class on class or a certain kind of uh  elite hubris then of course that  revolution is going to be incredibly  welcoming and of course  there are elements of all of these  things and you don't have to look far to  see them  but i'm going to argue that actually  commerce and  what can be called commercial society  have virtues and values of their own and  we need to reflect on those before we  think about revolutionary change that we  might want to impose on them  now if we think about society uh a  simple classification that would be  widespread might be a distinction into  agrarian or industrial societies uh a  distinction perhaps between uh  capitalist and uh communist societies  but of course there is seems to be a  kind of  fixed expectation in uh many people's  minds and perhaps in many countries that  the future in one form or another is  about some form of capitalism now i'm  not sure that's true  at all and for reasons that we'll come  to i'm not sure that's really what we  should be thinking about particularly  but it's worth just noting that there  are different varieties of capitalism  available around the world there are  what you might call countries with  western style  liberal democratic capitalism uh there  is china which combines communist  politics with  capitalist economics  there are countries that are based on  economies that are dominated by the  armed forces or in some cases by  particular interests might be drugs in  some cases in the past uh historically  or in some cases perhaps today  or it might be other forces that shape  the nature of the capitalism that they  have so even capitalism itself is not a  settled uh or unitary category and i  think it's important to note that up  front and of course how countries  achieve whatever state of development  they have may itself be wildly different  one from another there may be countries  that have come together as germany did  out of dutchies and principals in the  19th century uh there may be countries  that have  achieved or enjoyed a fairly stable  unitary political status as this country  has of course with disagreements over  the years since uh the early 17th  century  or the early 18th century depending on  how you think of it and of course there  are a host of different uh options and  different possibilities in between there  will be countries that have been uh and  still bear the legacy positive or  negative of colonialism of empire and  many other different pathways to the  current state that they have and these  things interplay with each other one  thing however that does seem to be  characteristic  of our current era is not just the  assumption that somehow capitalism  perhaps following the fall of the berlin  wall in 1989 is some form of  inevitability but also that the way to  think about these things is through  the lens of economics economics is the  mechanism by which we should  evaluate  the strength or well-being of given  societies and it's economics it should  be the  principle guiding tool when we come to  make trade-offs between uh people uh or  interests uh within countries and across  countries over time so that really is a  way of just setting the scene for  uh the argument that i want to make  uh now i want to start with a great hero  of mine i've written a book about him uh  adam smith uh of course not necessarily  familiar to everyone in his own uh in  his own full being um i think everyone  would think of him many people think of  him as the  father of  economics and  in the sense that economics comes of age  with the placing of markets  at the center uh of that body of thought  then i think that's an appropriate  description i mean there there is  economics plenty of economics before  smith  wrote the wealth of nations but all  economics afterwards bears its stamp in  different forms and it does so because  it is so emphatically focused on uh the  functioning of markets and the benefits  and of course the drawbacks  of markets uh as part of a wider scheme  of an understanding of how societies and  how economies develop  and for smith the crucial change that he  had seen uh in british history in  scottish history and that was powering  its way through in his own lifetime was  the transition from what he took to be  feudalism into commercial society now  what was that  change  well for smith feudalism is marked by  personal dependency so you might have a  lord of the manor or a great aristocrat  or indeed ultimately the king or queen  and arising from that source of  authority and power and wealth there  would be a relationships of a personal  dependence uh working their way through  society and you have of course in the  16th century and afterwards this idea of  the great chain of being that somehow  people's function is defined by where  they sit in society and that's all they  can ever respond to and all they should  aspire to  and that somehow that is divinely  licensed and uh ratified now that view  is one that he takes adam smith takes to  have been decisively superseded  uh in the uh 17th and 18th centuries and  that is through the move towards or  towards what he calls commercial society  and a commercial society for him is he  has a lovely phrase description of it he  says it's a society essentially in which  um every man of course he's only talking  about men as was the time the practice  of the time every man is in himself as  it were a merchant and lives by  exchanging so it's a society of exchange  and it's a society in which people in  the act of exchanging uh treat each  other as presumptive equals they may not  in fact be equals economically or  in terms of the hierarchy or status or  the other ways you might think about it  but they cheat and treat each other for  the purposes of exchange presumptively  as equal and therefore uh he sees that  as a much more  equal and indeed equalizing uh set of  social arrangements than feudalism and  one that's not marked by personal  dependency you need have no direct  personal relationship with someone if  you're exchanging adam smith one point  points to his coat and speculates on the  innumerable trades that may have been  involved in producing the dye and the  wool that it's made from and of course  he need have no knowledge of the people  who did the dyeing or generated the dye  itself or did the carving or the combing  or the weaving of the coat  and he doesn't need to because  the action of markets through society is  what guarantees uh the availability of a  code to him and ultimately it's quality  so  a commercial society need not be one  that relies on personal relationships as  such uh or indeed personal dependency  so that's the broad picture and what is  so interesting about that of course is  that uh somehow this idea of exchange  has been preoccupied in people's minds  by economic exchange and of course that  is a central part of the wealth of  nations but of course that misses out  what you might call its moral  dimension and for smith all exchange has  a moral dimension and uh he wrote a book  before he wrote the wealth of nations  the theory of moral sentiments which  talks about how that moral dimension to  our lives how value how norms  are generated  in society and what he says is  extraordinarily interesting he says that  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai dot tv  for more debates and talks from the  world's leading thinkers on today's  biggest ideas

